Replay the scrollbar slider only into the current drawable

The GTK 2 engine paints the scrollbar slider a second time when it
paints the last stepper of the same scrollbar. The second paint uses
the drawable that the engine stored at the first slider paint. The
stored pointer holds no reference. A host that paints each scrollbar
part into its own drawable can free that drawable before the stepper
paint. The type check at the start of gtkDrawSlider then reads freed
memory. Pale Moon crashes there.

The stepper paint now compares its own drawable with the stored one.
The engine replays the slider only when the two are the same, and it
clears the stored record in both cases. GTK paints the slider and the
steppers of one scrollbar into one window, so the replay stays as it
was for every GTK application.

A reference on the stored drawable keeps the old pixmap alive, and
the replay still paints into that old pixmap. A weak pointer removes
the crash but keeps that wrong paint. The compare removes both.

BUG: 411019

Accept a NULL constraint rectangle in Rect::constrain

GTK 2 passes a NULL area to gtk_paint_box when the caller sets no
clip. The engine passes that area to Rect::constrain for the plain
progress stripes. Rect::constrain forwards it to Rect::intersect,
which reads the rectangle. The engine reads address zero and the
application stops with SIGSEGV. QGtkStyle paints a QProgressBar
this way. The Application Style page of System Settings therefore
crashed when the GTK 2 theme was QtCurve with plain stripes.

The helper that Rect::constrain replaced returned without a change
when the constraint was NULL. Rect::constrain now does the same,
and its doc comment states the contract. A NULL area means no clip
in GTK 2, so the stripe rectangles keep their full size. The result
is the same as with an area that covers the whole widget. A
rectangle with a constraint gets the same result as before. The
entry progress bar shares the function and gets the same protection.

BUG: 427950

Remove the early window when a widget becomes a child

With a background, dialog or menu opacity below 100, prePolish calls
addAlphaChannel on every top-level widget before Qt creates its native
window. addAlphaChannel makes the QWidgetWindow early, so the alpha
format request reaches the platform window. Qt adopts that window when
it creates a window or a native child. Qt does not adopt it when the
widget became a child before creation: the child keeps the window.

Qt 6 ignores the unused window. On Qt 5 the child keeps a
pointer to freed memory after the parent widget loses its own
window. QWidgetPrivate::show_sys crashes on the next show. A floating
dock widget and a widget made without a parent and then put into a
layout reach this state.

The event callback now calls removeAlphaWindow on every ParentChange
event of a widget without a native window. The function deletes the
early window when the widget is not a window and has no WA_NativeWindow
attribute. It uses the private QWidgetPrivate interface that made
the window. A widget that becomes a top-level again gets a new early
window from prePolish at the same event.

The early creation stays. Qt sends no event between its own creation
of the QWidgetWindow and the creation of the platform window. The
alpha format can reach the platform window only through a window that
exists before QWidget::create. A skip of Qt::Tool widgets would keep
the fault for every parentless widget of type Qt::Window.

BUG: 429201

Do not overwrite the blur region that Konsole sets

Konsole sets the blur region of its main window when it applies a
profile. The blur helper of the style claimed that window as one of
its own translucent windows. On each show and resize it replaced the
region with the window rectangle less the opaque terminal widget.
The terminal then lost its blur until the next profile switch.

The helper now lists Konsole::MainWindow with the windows that it
must not touch, beside Plasma::Dialog. The old clause that named
the class as a transparent window is gone. That removal alone is
not enough. The style sets WA_StyledBackground on every main window
when the window background is translucent. That flag matched
the Konsole window as well. The exclusion list is the place the
helper already uses for a window whose application owns the blur
region.

Windows of every other class keep the old behaviour.

BUG: 401869

Use the icon theme for message box and file dialog icons

A build without KDE support returned the compiled-in images for the
message box icons and never asked the icon theme. The icons then did
not match the icon theme of the desktop. A build with KDE support
asked the theme for "go-edn" and "go-end". The freedesktop names are
"go-first" and "go-last". No theme has the two wrong names. The start
icon in a right-to-left layout and the end icon in a left-to-right
layout were null.

Ask the theme for "dialog-information", "dialog-warning" and
"dialog-error" in the build without KDE support. Keep the compiled-in
image as the fallback, so a session without an icon theme shows the
same icons as before. Correct the two wrong names to "go-last".

The two-argument QIcon::fromTheme takes the fallback only when the
theme has no such icon, so one call covers both cases. The question
icon keeps the information icon, as the build with KDE support and
the GTK 2 icon map already do.

BUG: 337151

Paint a popup menu from the palette of the menu

The style fills a popup menu from a cache of menu shades. The cache
follows the palette that Qt gives to polish(QPalette&). That is the
base palette of the platform theme. QApplication::setPalette() sets a
palette that Qt resolves over the base palette, and polish(QPalette&)
sees the base palette alone. The menu text takes its colour from the
palette of the menu, and that palette follows setPalette(). After an
application applies a dark palette over a light theme, the menu keeps
the light fill and gets light text.

polish(QWidget*) also writes the cached colour into the Window role of
each menu. Qt keeps a role that a widget sets, so a later palette
change does not reach a menu that already exists.

popupMenuCols(option) now builds the menu shades from the window
colour of the option palette when that colour differs from the cached
window colour. It applies the lighter factor of the cache. A widget
whose palette already holds the cached menu colour keeps the cache.
The menu panel, the menu scroller and the combo box container take
their fill through the option. polish(QWidget*) no longer writes the
cached colour into a menu. The write stays for a menu that takes the
menu bar colour, because the palette does not hold that colour.

Buttons, menu bars and window backgrounds already follow this rule: a
widget whose palette differs from the application palette gets shades
of its own colour.

BUG: 446937

Do not stroke a square outline on a rounded tooltip

With square tooltips off and a flat tooltip appearance, the Qt
styles fill a rounded path. They then stroke a square outline on
the full rectangle. The window mask of an opaque tooltip cuts the
corners of the window. The outline then stops before each corner,
and the window below shows in the gap. A tooltip window with an
alpha channel gets no mask, so the square corners of the outline
stand outside the rounded fill. The antialiasing of the rounded
fill also puts the outline on two half pixels, and only one half
is inside the window.

The Qt styles now stroke the outline only when the tooltip is
square, as the GTK 2 engine does in drawToolTip(). A rounded
tooltip keeps its fill path and its window mask. A square tooltip
keeps its full outline. The default configuration has square
tooltips and does not change.

BUG: 336665

Keep the combo popup mask when no compositor runs

The rounded combo popup gets an alpha channel at polish time. The
Resize and Show branches of the event filter then clear the window
mask when the window format has an alpha channel. An X server
without a compositor ignores the alpha channel. It shows the
unpainted corner pixels of the popup as black.

The two branches now clear the mask only when a compositor is
active. Without one, they set the staircase mask that an opaque
popup already gets, and the corners show the window below.

The check is local to the two branches. The other callers of
hasAlphaChannel paint the window content with the alpha channel,
and a change there has a wider effect. The window format does not
change, so a compositor that starts later gets the antialiased
corners at the next show. The staircase mask does not change: a
window shape has no partial pixel.

BUG: 336664

Fit the round paths to the box and the pixel grid

The split path of a bevel takes the radius of the widget. For a
radio button, a dial, a round title bar button and a round slider
that radius is half the widget width. Under the round max option a
scroll bar slider takes half of its short side, up to a cap. The
path box is one pixel smaller than the widget. The four quarter
arcs therefore came from four circles that were one pixel apart.
The joins between the arcs made a bright knot at each axis end of
the coloured hover ring and of the etch ring.

The two hover arcs of the plain radio button sat on integer
coordinates. A one pixel pen on an integer coordinate covers two
pixel rows at half strength. The ring was a soft band with a dent
at each axis end.

Limit the arc diameter of the split path to the smaller side of
its box. The Qt styles and the cairo path helpers of the GTK 2
style get the same limit. The four arcs then lie on one circle
for a square box, as the whole path of the border in the Qt
styles already does. Move the two hover arcs of the plain radio
button to the half pixel grid. Their centre stays and their radius
shrinks by half a pixel. The pen then covers whole pixels on the
axes.

A rounded rectangle keeps its path when its diameter fits the
path box. In the GTK 2 style the fill clip of a radio button takes
the same limit, so that button also changes at rest.

BUG: 336660

Paint the tab hover glow under the tab border

The mouse-over glow of an inactive tab was a one pixel ring
painted after the border, one pixel outside it. At the rounded
corners the two antialiased strokes share pixels. The glow pass
scaled the dark border coverage of those pixels by its own
alpha, so the outline looked pale and frayed in the corners.

A pen of width one also gives less coverage on an arc than the
area of the stroke. That loss is the same in every style, and
this change does not touch it.

The four tab shapes now paint the glow before the border. The
glow is a two pixel ring on the integer grid that covers the
border pixel and the pixel outside it. The opaque border hides
the inner pixel of the ring, so the straight edges do not change.
Where the border stroke is partial, it blends with glow and not
with background, as an exact render of the two rings does. The
wider pen keeps its coverage on the arc, so the glow ring itself
does not fade at the corners.

The glow of buttons and other widgets keeps its painter.

BUG: 336662

The changes sit on the branch work/bug-sweep of the local tree, as
commits 1 to 10 of 10. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge
request !31 on system/qtcurve holds that branch. The tenth change
follows the etchBottomCol block that the patch style-paint-01 adds.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

